 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order to `t1…tn` and resets `next` to `n+1`, so keys stay
+  contiguous (`t1,t2,t3`) across opens and closes.
+- Rejected: leaving a gap after a close (`t1,t3`), the former §stable-tab-keys (since 2026-09-02), overridden by the
+  maintainer on 2026-10-22.
+- Reason: maintainer decision. Accepted cost: the key is the React key and the URL state, so every tab to the right
+  of a closed one remounts and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (the 2026-08-29 incident behaviour),
+  and a key held in a saved URL can point at a different tab after a close.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close stays contiguous")
